## Phishing Analysis

Notes for SOC alert triage and email investigation.


## Part 1 — The Psychological Levers

Every phish works by exploiting a human decision shortcut, not a technical flaw. Identifying _which_ lever an email pulls is often faster than analyzing the headers, and it's what you write in the ticket to explain why a user clicked.

|Lever|How it works|What it looks like in an email|
|---|---|---|
|**Authority**|People comply with perceived power|Spoofed CEO, IT department, HR, law enforcement, IRS|
|**Urgency**|Time pressure suppresses verification|"Expires in 24 hours," "immediate action required," "final notice"|
|**Fear**|Threat of loss or consequence|Account suspension, legal action, termination, security breach|
|**Trust / Familiarity**|Impersonating a known brand or person|Microsoft 365 login, DocuSign, a coworker's display name|
|**Scarcity**|Limited availability drives fast action|"Only 3 slots left," limited-time bonus|
|**Social proof**|People follow the group|"Your team has already signed," shared-document notifications|
|**Curiosity**|Unresolved information gap|"Here's that photo of you," unlabeled attachment, missed voicemail|
|**Greed / Reward**|Promise of gain|Refund owed, bonus, gift card, crypto giveaway|
|**Helpfulness / Reciprocity**|People want to assist, especially new hires|"Can you do me a quick favor?" — a classic BEC opener|

**Analyst note:** most real phishing stacks two or three of these. The strongest combination is **Authority + Urgency + Fear** — an executive demanding something immediately with a consequence attached. If you see all three in one message, treat it as hostile until proven otherwise.

