## Email Headers

Headers are metadata prepended to the message body. Everything above the first blank line is header; everything below is body.

### Reading the Received chain

**Received headers stack newest on top.** Each server prepends its own as the message passes through. To trace the real path, **read from the bottom up** — the bottom-most `Received:` is the earliest hop, closest to the true origin.

```
Received: from mx.recipient.com ...        ← last hop (newest)
Received: from relay.middle.net ...        ← middle hop
Received: from unknown-host [203.0.113.5]  ← first hop (oldest, origin)
```

What to extract from each hop: the sending IP, the hostname the sender _claimed_ in HELO/EHLO, the hostname reverse DNS actually resolves to, and the timestamp.

**Red flags in the chain:**

- HELO/EHLO hostname doesn't match the reverse DNS of the connecting IP
- A gap or inconsistency in timestamps (hops out of chronological order)
- Originating IP geolocates somewhere inconsistent with the claimed sender
- Forged `Received:` headers injected at the bottom to fake an origin — **only the headers added by servers you control are fully trustworthy**, since anything upstream could have been fabricated by the attacker

That last point matters more than people expect. Trust the headers your own infrastructure wrote. Treat everything below them as a claim.

### Headers that matter in triage

|Header|What it tells you|
|---|---|
|`From:`|Displayed sender. Trivially spoofable.|
|`Return-Path:`|Envelope sender, recorded at delivery. Where bounces go. Compare against `From:`.|
|`Reply-To:`|Where a reply is actually addressed. **A mismatch here is a classic BEC indicator.**|
|`Received:`|The hop-by-hop path. Read bottom-up.|
|`Message-ID:`|Unique identifier, usually `<random@sending-domain>`. A domain here that doesn't match the sender is suspicious.|
|`Authentication-Results:`|Your own mail system's SPF, DKIM, and DMARC verdicts. High-trust — your infrastructure wrote it.|
|`DKIM-Signature:`|Contains `d=` (signing domain) and `s=` (selector). Check whether `d=` aligns with `From:`.|
|`Date:`|Claimed send time. Compare against Received timestamps.|
|`Subject:`|Content, but also check for encoded text (`=?UTF-8?B?`) hiding the real subject.|
|`Content-Type:`|MIME structure — `multipart/mixed` means attachments present.|
|`X-Mailer:` / `User-Agent:`|Sending client. Bulk mailers and scripts often leave distinctive values here.|
|`X-Originating-IP:`|Original sender IP, when the provider includes it.|
|`List-Unsubscribe:`|Present on legitimate bulk mail; its _absence_ on something claiming to be a newsletter is a signal.|

### MIME

Email was originally plain ASCII text only. **MIME** (Multipurpose Internet Mail Extensions) is what allows attachments, HTML, and non-English character sets.

- `Content-Type: multipart/mixed` — message has attachments
- `Content-Type: multipart/alternative` — plain text and HTML versions of the same content
- `boundary=` — the delimiter string separating each MIME part
- `Content-Transfer-Encoding: base64` — binary content encoded as text; this is how attachments travel

**Analyst note:** HTML email lets the displayed link text differ completely from the actual href. `Click here to verify` pointing somewhere entirely different is the oldest trick in the book and still the most common. Always read the raw href, never the rendered text.

